<%# HOW ONE POOL IS DOING, IN THE APP'S ONE ROW VOCABULARY (spec §4.4). Local: `row` — anything
    answering #status, #period_closed?, #changed_after_distributing?, #needs_attention?,
    #due_marker? and #balance_clause?. Today: `HomePresenter::Row`, `BudgetPagePresenter::Group`
    and `CategoryBudgetPresenter`.

    WHY THIS PARTIAL EXISTS, AND IT IS NOT DE-DUPLICATION. `pool_status_label` takes BOTH suffixes
    as optional keywords, so every caller of it is free to thread one and forget the other, and a
    forgotten suffix is silent — the row still renders, just describing the same envelope
    differently from the screen next to it. That defect shipped twice: once between Home and
    /budget (`_pool_group` passed `period_closed:` alone) and once between Home's OWN two bands
    (`pool_problem_label` passed `changed_after_distributing:` alone). Both were caught in review,
    neither by a test, because there is nothing to fail — the assertion has to already suspect the
    missing clause.

    So the threading happens ONCE, here, off ONE object. A caller no longer chooses which suffixes
    to pass; it chooses which OBJECT to pass, and an object that cannot answer all four questions
    raises NoMethodError the first time the row renders. That is the difference between "currently
    consistent" and "structurally hard to drift".

    THE INDENTATION BELOW IS LOAD-BEARING and deliberately does not read as nested markup. This
    partial replaced the identical span that stood in `home/_pool_row` and
    `budget_page/_pool_group`, and the refactor's contract was byte-identical rendering on both
    screens — so the literal whitespace those two files emitted lives here now, at the column each
    caller's own render line puts it. Both render this from four spaces in; that is why the closing
    tag sits at four and the contents at six. Moving a caller changes what this file has to say.
    There is no trailing newline for the same reason: the caller's own line supplies it.

    THE CLAUSE AFTER THE STATE IS THE ROW'S, not this file's. Home adds a date, the Budget page
    adds `· holds $X`, and neither is an accident of markup — a Home row shows no rules and no
    balance anywhere else on the line, while a Budget card lists every rule beneath it and is
    missing only the money. The row says which it wants; this partial does not ask which screen it
    is on. %>
<span class="text-sm <%= row.needs_attention? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-700" %>">
      <%= pool_status_label(row.status,
                            period_closed: row.period_closed?,
                            changed_after_distributing: row.changed_after_distributing?) %>
<% if row.due_marker? %>        · <%= row.status.due_on.strftime("%b %-d") %>
<% end %><% if row.balance_clause? %>      <span class="text-gray-400"><%= pool_balance_clause(row.status) %></span>
<% end %>    </span>